<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#aquifer">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aquifer</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An aquifer is an underground layer of rock or sediment that holds water. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> depended on fossil aquifers tapped by underground channels called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foggara">foggaras</a> to irrigate its oases, and their depletion is linked to its decline. In Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanat</a> systems drawing on aquifers likewise supported agriculture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aquifer.jpg" alt="Aquifer" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T.C. Winter, J.W. Harvey, O.L. Franke, and W.M. Alley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Groundwater_flow.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> prefix aqui-, a form of aqua, water, and the suffix -fer, bearing. An aquifer is an underground layer of permeable, porous or fractured rock, or of unconsolidated gravel, sand or silt, that holds water. Aquifers are sometimes described as underground rivers or lakes, but they are in fact porous rock saturated with water. They occur from near the surface to depths of more than 9,000 metres, and those nearer the surface are more likely to be replenished by local rainfall.</p><p>Aquifers are classified in several ways. A confined aquifer lies under an impermeable barrier, often clay, while an unconfined aquifer has none directly above it and its water level can rise with recharge. An aquitard is a bed of low permeability that restricts the flow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> from one aquifer to another. Porous aquifers occur in sand and sandstone, karst aquifers develop in limestone as acidic water enlarges fissures, and a rock of low porosity can still serve if it is highly fractured. The study of water flow in aquifers is called hydrogeology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">Groundwater</a> is drawn from aquifers through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and used for drinking water, agriculture and industry. Many desert areas have limestone hills or mountains that can be exploited as groundwater resources: shallow aquifers are used in parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and Anti-Lebanon ranges, and the Jebel Akhdar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. Some aquifers hold fossil water from the last glaciation, as the Ogallala Aquifer of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> does, and where more water is taken out than is replenished the practical yield is exceeded.</p><p>Overdrafting can lower the water table, and along coasts such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and Israel it has let sea water contaminate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>. Among the largest aquifers are the Great Artesian Basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, over 1.7 million square kilometres, and the Guarani Aquifer beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Hydrogeology has to establish how fast and in what direction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> travels. In porous aquifers water seeps slowly between sand grains, and a flow of 1 foot a day is a high rate. In karst aquifers dye traces in the Barton Springs Edwards aquifer measured flows of 0.5 to 7 miles a day, which makes them much more open to contamination. In 2013 large aquifers of low-salinity water were reported under the continental shelves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, formed when sea levels were lower, before the end of the ice age 20,000 years ago.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aquifer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the later first millennium BCE, they developed the foggara, gently inclined underground channels tapping the fossil water stored in the sandstone <b>aquifers</b> beneath the Fezzan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Iranian agriculture depended on the qanat system of underground channels carrying water from mountain <b>aquifers</b> to lowland fields.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
